# SwiftFormat config for /ios — brew-installed `swiftformat` (0.63.x).
#
# Run from /ios, pointing explicitly at this file so a developer's global
# ~/.swiftformat is not merged in (keeps formatting hermetic to the repo):
#
#   swiftformat --config .swiftformat .            # format
#   swiftformat --lint --config .swiftformat .     # check only, no writes
#
# Rationale lives in /CLAUDE.md § Framework Conventions → Swift formatting.
# The load-bearing choices:
#   1. Fixed one-indent continuation — never align to an open paren.
#   2. The AUTHOR decides whether a parameter/argument list is one-line or
#      one-item-per-line; SwiftFormat only normalizes the shape you chose
#      (indent, trailing comma, ")" placement) and force-wraps a line that
#      exceeds --maxwidth. It never collapses a multi-line list back to one
#      line. So: write it on one line if it should be one line; break it
#      after "(" with one item per line if each deserves its own line.

--swiftversion 6.2

--indent 4
--tabwidth 4
--maxwidth 120

# When a list is wrapped (by the author, or by exceeding --maxwidth): break
# after "(", one item per line, ")" on its own line.
--wrapparameters before-first
--wraparguments before-first
--wrapcollections before-first
--closingparen balanced

# Kills Xcode's align-continuation-to-the-open-paren behavior. This is
# SwiftFormat's default; pinned here so the convention is explicit.
--xcodeindentation disabled

# With before-first wrapping, a trailing comma makes adding an argument a
# one-line diff. Argument-list trailing commas require Swift 6.1+ (we're 6.2).
--trailingcommas always

# Authoring choice, not formatting: don't rewrite regular `//` comments into
# `///` doc comments.
--disable docComments
